  "worked_solution": "In cylindrical polar coordinates, the given half-cone has the equation\n\n$$\nz=r.\n$$\n\nIf $(r,\\theta ,z)$ are the cylindrical polar coordinates of a point, then its $(x,y,z)$ coordinates can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,z=z.\n$$\n\nNow, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r,\\theta ) & =\u27e8x,y,z\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,z\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta ,\\,r\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ,\\,r\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $r\\in [0,\\infty )$ and $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nTherefore, the first component of $f$ is $r\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta .$",
